<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernabò Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1323">1323</a>–<a href="/years/1385">1385</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernabò Visconti was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, ruling the eastern half of the Visconti <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> from 1354 while his brother Galeazzo II held the west. Known for his cruelty and his constant wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, he governed from Milan. In 1385 his nephew Gian Galeazzo seized him by a ruse and imprisoned him at Trezzo, where he died months later, reportedly poisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti.jpg" alt="Bernabò Visconti" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko, edited by MenkinAlRire · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonino_da_Campione,_monumento_sepolcrale_di_Bernab%C3%B2_Visconti,_1385-90_ca.,_da_s._Giovanni_in_Conca_02-edit-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bernabò was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, the son of Stefano Visconti and Valentina Doria, and was named after his maternal grandfather Bernabò Doria, once ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. His paternal grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-i-visconti">Matteo I Visconti</a> was in conflict with the Church, and the whole family was excommunicated on a charge of heresy the year before Bernabò's birth. His father died in 1327, and the children were brought up by their mother. From 1346 to 1349 Bernabò lived in exile, until his uncle Giovanni Visconti called him back to Milan after the death of Luchino. On 27 September 1350 he married Beatrice Regina della Scala, daughter of Mastino II of Verona.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>At Giovanni's death in 1354 Bernabò inherited power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> with his brothers Matteo and Galeazzo, taking the eastern lands of Bergamo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, Cremona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crema">Crema</a>. Matteo died in 1355, rumoured to have been poisoned by his brothers, who divided his inheritance. His intrigues kept him almost continuously at war with Pope Urban V, the Florentines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and Savoy. In 1360 Pope Innocent VI declared him a heretic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, and he was defeated at San Ruffillo on 29 July 1361 by imperial troops under Galeotto I Malatesta. He was excommunicated again on 4 March 1363, and the ban was lifted under the peace of 13 March 1364 in exchange for 500,000 florins.</p><p>In 1373 the pope sent two delegates to serve Bernabò and Galeazzo their excommunication papers, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parchment">parchment</a> with a leaden seal rolled in a silken cord. The furious Bernabò arrested them and refused to release them until they had eaten the parchment, seal and cord. In 1370 he ordered the Trezzo Bridge, then the largest single-arch bridge in the world. He joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, and his troops were defeated in the Val Bisagno in September 1379.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His despotism and taxes enraged the Milanese, and Chaucer's Monk's Tale includes him among tyrants brought down by Fortune. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> deposed him on 6 May 1385. He was imprisoned in the castle of Trezzo, where he died on 19 December of that year, presumably poisoned. Bonino da Campione sculpted his equestrian statue around 1363 for the church of San Giovanni in Conca; it was reused as his funerary monument and is now in the Castello Sforzesco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. He had at least fifteen legitimate children, and several daughters married into the royal and ducal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernabò Visconti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1385 Gian Galeazzo Visconti seized sole power by arresting and imprisoning his uncle <b>Bernabò</b>, and in 1395 he purchased the title of duke from the King of the Romans, Wenceslas, for a large sum, reportedly 100,000 florins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Gian Galeazzo Visconti overthrew his uncle <b>Bernabò</b> in 1385 and created a centralised state, beginning Milan Cathedral in 1386.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Trezzo sull'Adda, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1363" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bernabò Visconti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1363">What was here in 1363? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
